#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <poll.h>
#include <sys/select.h>
int main(void){struct timeval t={0,0}; return poll(0,0,0)!=0 || select(0,0,0,0,&t)!=0;}

Phase 12 record (std-progress.md, Phase 12) and retained next-blocker.log read before edits.
Historical operations: poll(NULL,0,0); select(0,NULL,NULL,NULL,&zero_timeval).
No struct pollfd instantiation or field/layout check; no event flags referenced.
Only zero/nonblocking timeout tested. Negative/infinite and positive millisecond
waits are NOT tested by the historical probe. No pipe, stdio, socket, invalid,
closed, or negative descriptor is passed. These are separate contract coverage.

Native syscall 159 previously required 1..3 pipe-read/TTY descriptors,
events exactly POLLIN and timeout >= -1; zero fds failed. Finite timeout
replayed SYSCALL without retaining the absolute deadline, restarting the wait.
Linux poll and ppoll exist only via compatibility syscall remapping (1006/1047).
The native bridge uses 159 and retains native personality and capabilities.
C pollfd is identical to the existing repr(C) PollFd / IPC NativePollFd:
size 8, alignment 4, fd:i32 at 0, events:i16 at 4, revents:i16 at 6.
nfds_t is unsigned long, 64-bit unsigned on LP64.

Readiness inspection: pipe pool exposes data length and reader/writer reference
counts; TTY readiness uses the input ring; stdout/stderr are writable;
synchronous VFS operations cannot park and regular-file EOF remains readable.
Native eventfd readiness uses the existing counter hook; eventfd and TTY wakes
now include native poll waiters. Socket and epoll handle polling is rejected
with EINVAL pending a complete native event/hangup contract, not faked ready.
POLLIN/POLLOUT readiness and unconditional POLLERR/POLLHUP/POLLNVAL supported.
POLLPRI is defined but has no native producer; no exceptional readiness claimed.
No native ppoll/pselect, signal mask, or EINTR implementation added.
